{#-
  RFC-0089 / phase-429 W1 — the CODEGEN VERSION STAMP emitted into every
  generated C and C++ header. This block is a jinja comment: it explains the
  mechanism to whoever edits the pack, and does NOT land in the artifact, which
  keeps a ~30-line rationale out of every generated file in a user's tree.

  MECHANISM. The artifact references `nros_codegen_version_v<G>`, G being the
  version it was emitted at. The runtime DEFINES one weak
  `nros_codegen_version_v<K>` for every K in
  [NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION_MIN, NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION] — see
  packages/tooling/nros-build-helpers/csrc/codegen_version_symbols.c. So the
  accepted range IS the set of defined symbols: nothing compares two numbers
  anywhere, so there is no comparison that could itself be wrong. A generated
  tree the runtime does not accept fails to LINK, naming the version it wanted,
  instead of linking cleanly and disagreeing about layout at run time.

  IDEMPOTENT. The `#ifndef` guard means N generated headers in one translation
  unit produce ONE anchor; a TU mixing two codegen versions gets one anchor each
  and fails on whichever the runtime lacks.

  LANGUAGE-NEUTRAL on purpose — the stamp is about the C ABI, which is exactly
  the surface the C and C++ packs share, so both packs include this one file
  rather than each carrying a spelling of it.

  CARRIER, chosen deliberately. C has a single guaranteed carrier (every
  generated C artifact includes <nros/types.h>), but C++ has none: message.hpp
  includes six nros/*.hpp, service.hpp and action.hpp only two. The per-package
  umbrella <pkg>/<pkg>.hpp was the other candidate and is WEAKER — a consumer
  that includes only <pkg>/msg/Foo.hpp never reaches it, and a header nobody
  includes stamps nothing. So the stamp goes in EVERY generated header of both
  languages, and the guard above is what keeps that from costing more than one
  anchor per translation unit. C++ generated output is headers-only, so a header
  is also the only place a C++ artifact CAN carry it.
-#}
/* RFC-0089 — emitted by nano-ros codegen version {{ codegen_version }}. The runtime defines
 * one weak `nros_codegen_version_v<K>` per version it accepts, so a runtime
 * that does not accept {{ codegen_version }} fails to LINK this artifact and names the
 * version. Regenerate with `nros sync` against the runtime you are building. */
#ifndef NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION_ANCHOR_V{{ codegen_version }}
#define NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION_ANCHOR_V{{ codegen_version }}
#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C" const unsigned char nros_codegen_version_v{{ codegen_version }};
#else
extern const unsigned char nros_codegen_version_v{{ codegen_version }};
#endif
#if defined(__GNUC__) || defined(__clang__)
__attribute__((used, unused))
static const unsigned char *const nros__codegen_version_anchor_v{{ codegen_version }} =
    &nros_codegen_version_v{{ codegen_version }};
#endif
#endif /* NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION_ANCHOR_V{{ codegen_version }} */
